# Chapter 41: WebAssembly (WASM) as a First-Class Target

WebAssembly is no longer a curiosity—it is the universal bytecode of the modern web, embedded runtimes, and plugin systems. Zig treats `wasm32-freestanding` and `wasm32-wasi` as first-class targets, not as afterthoughts bolted on with a separate toolchain. The same compiler, the same standard library (subject to platform availability), and the same explicit philosophy translate directly into a linear memory model that feels native to a systems programmer. This chapter shows how to build, export, interface, and think about Zig when the target is WebAssembly.

---

## 1. Basic Usage

Compiling Zig to WebAssembly requires nothing more than a target triple. For a library that will be consumed by JavaScript or another host, you typically want a freestanding dynamic object (a “reactor” in WASI terminology, or a bare module). The incantation with the build system is:

```zig
// build.zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn build(b: *std.Build) void {
    const target = b.resolveTargetQuery(.{
        .cpu_arch = .wasm32,
        .os_tag = .freestanding,
    });
    const optimize = b.standardOptimizeOption(.{});

    const lib = b.addSharedLibrary(.{
        .name = "mylib",
        .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
        .target = target,
        .optimize = optimize,
    });
    // No entry point; we are exporting functions only.
    lib.entry = .disabled;
    b.installArtifact(lib);
}
```

Now, inside `src/main.zig`, export a function with the C calling convention:

```zig
export fn add(a: i32, b: i32) callconv(.C) i32 {
    return a + b;
}
```

The `callconv(.C)` is mandatory. The default Zig calling convention is unstable and specific to the Zig compiler version—it cannot be consumed by external hosts. The C ABI is the lingua franca for WASM exports.

To use the resulting `mylib.wasm` from JavaScript:

```js
const wasm = await WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(
    fetch('mylib.wasm')
);
const { add } = wasm.instance.exports;
console.log(add(2, 3)); // 5
```

When your library needs to allocate memory that JavaScript will read, you must export an allocator interface. At its simplest, use a bump-pointer allocator backed by the WASM linear memory and expose `alloc` and `dealloc` (or `free`).

```zig
const std = @import("std");

var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
const allocator = gpa.allocator();

export fn alloc(len: usize) callconv(.C) ?[*]u8 {
    const slice = allocator.alloc(u8, len) catch return null;
    return slice.ptr;
}

export fn dealloc(ptr: [*]u8, len: usize) callconv(.C) void {
    allocator.free(ptr[0..len]);
}
```

JavaScript can then create a view into the WASM memory, call `alloc`, fill the buffer, and call your processing function:

```js
const memory = wasm.instance.exports.memory;
const bufPtr = wasm.instance.exports.alloc(inputStr.length);
const buf = new Uint8Array(memory.buffer, bufPtr, inputStr.length);
for (let i = 0; i < inputStr.length; i++) buf[i] = inputStr.charCodeAt(i);
wasm.instance.exports.process(bufPtr, inputStr.length);
// read output...
wasm.instance.exports.dealloc(bufPtr, inputStr.length);
```

This pattern—explicit allocation, manual memory copying, no hidden serialization—is the Zig Way applied to WASM.

**WASI mode** is chosen when you want a more POSIX-like environment (file access, environment variables, standard streams). Use `-target wasm32-wasi` and a normal entry point:

```zig
pub fn main() !void {
    const stdout = std.io.getStdOut().writer();
    try stdout.print("Hello, WASI!\n", .{});
}
```

The build system step is identical, except the target becomes `.os_tag = .wasi`. WASI implies the presence of a libc-like layer provided by the host, but Zig uses its own WASI headers and links against the host’s WASI implementation.

