## 7. Best Practices

**Design the API boundary first.** Before writing Zig code, define the interface table: which functions are exported, what arguments they take, and how memory ownership works. Prefer returning results in caller-provided buffers. If dynamic sizing is unavoidable, export `alloc`/`free` or a combined `alloc_thing`/`free_thing`.

**Use `FixedBufferAllocator` for no-dynamic-growth.** For many browser use cases, you know the maximum input size (e.g., a 4096x4096 image buffer). Statically allocate a buffer and never touch `memory.grow`. This keeps the WASM module simple and avoids the detached-buffer problem.

**Separate platform logic with comptime.** Write a platform-independent core that operates on slices, allocators, and IO-agnostic interfaces. For WASM, implement the IO layer with `extern "env"` functions. For native testing, use real files or sockets. This way, 90% of your code is testable with `zig test`.

**Manage the view into memory carefully on the JS side.** Wrap the WASM instance in a JavaScript class that provides typed views and refreshes them after any call that could grow memory. A clean pattern:

```js
class WasmModule {
    constructor(instance) {
        this.instance = instance;
        this.memory = instance.exports.memory;
        this.updateViews();
    }
    updateViews() {
        this.u8 = new Uint8Array(this.memory.buffer);
        this.i32 = new Int32Array(this.memory.buffer);
    }
    callExport(name, ...args) {
        const result = this.instance.exports[name](...args);
        this.updateViews();
        return result;
    }
}
```

**Use WASI for command-line tools.** If your Zig program is meant to run as a standalone WASI binary (e.g., in Node.js with `--experimental-wasi-unstable-preview1`, or in wasmtime), use `wasm32-wasi`. It gives you `std.fs`, `std.process`, and `std.log`. The same binary often runs unchanged on multiple hosts.

**Test early and often.** Use `zig test` with a `test` block that invokes your exported functions (they are just Zig functions, callable from test code). For integration tests, use a WASM runtime like `wasmtime` or Node.js with a simple test harness. Zig’s standard testing allocator can be used, but remember that it may leak detect, which is useful for finding mismatched frees.

